From a2f7fef6ada635bd4c48ecda51ced5b92fc9d7bf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 8 Sep 2026 08:59:19 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] fix(search): take an item's binary codes with the passages a
 catch-up clears (#30)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

deleteItem drops a whole item's codes before its passages go, because codes are
keyed by rowid and after the delete there is nothing left to name them by. Its
two partial siblings did not. clearFulltext and clearOwnWords remove one item's
body passages, or its notes and annotations, together with the vectors those
passages carry, and left every code taken from them behind.

Those two are the catch-up passes an action:"update" runs. Opening a PDF makes
Zotero extract its text and touches no item version (#26); writing a note
versions the child and not the parent (#33); so these are the only paths that
reach either kind of text, and both replace the item's passages wholesale. One
update over an index that carries codes therefore leaves one orphan code per
replaced passage, permanently: refreshCodes only writes codes for vectors that
have none, so nothing later removes them. loadCodes counts one code per stored
vector, finds more codes than vectors, and declines — every semantic query goes
back to the exact scan the codes exist to avoid, on every query, until someone
runs a full action:"build". Nothing reports it beyond the scan notice on
action:"status", which is why this can sit unnoticed on a working index.

The delete is scoped by source rather than reusing deleteItemCodes. That
statement takes every code of the item, and clearFulltext deliberately leaves
the item's metadata passages in place: stripping their codes would exchange a
surplus for a shortfall and decline the coded path just the same, for rows
nothing touched.

invalidateCodes() is called too, for the same reason every other path that
removes or replaces a vector calls it. The resident cache names rowids, and
between a clear and the update's own finalizeVectors there is an await on the
embedder: a query arriving in that window would otherwise be answered from a
cache naming rows that no longer exist, and stage one would score candidates
chosen on the sign bits of text this index no longer holds. Rebuilding the
cache is one pass over a table sized 384 bytes a passage, and putVector already
invalidates once per embedded passage, so this adds no cost a build did not
already pay.

Two cases, red before this change: a full-text catch-up and an own-words
catch-up over a 600-item index above the candidate floor, each asserting that
the codes still cover the vectors exactly, that no code names a passage the
index no longer holds, and that the next semantic query is still served by the
codes rather than by the scan.

A third case was written and dropped: a catch-up on the LAST item indexed,
where SQLite hands the cleared rowid straight back and the replacement inherits
its predecessor's code with the counts still matching. It passes without this
change, because putVector resolves a passage id to its rowid and deletes
whatever code sits there before writing the new vector. The rowid-reuse hazard
the deleteItemCodes comment warns about is real for a passage that is never
re-embedded, and that case is caught by the count check like any other orphan.

